th-conditional avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lucy speaks while “clinging to him” after he becomes absorbed in kissing her.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes intimate and emotionally intense delivery, but by itself does not establish independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She is simultaneously “making secret vows of sensibleness, of wholesomeness, of a determined, unfailing future simplicity.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit vows provide moderate support for commitment or undertaking force associated with the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Lucy is distressed by the garden, then tells herself that “Perfect simplicity” is the right way to meet the situation.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is contextually connected to self-persuasion and an effort to adapt herself, rather than being only an uncomplicated report of feeling.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Wemyss reflects that Lucy's face is “his very own for ever” and that she has duties regarding it while he has joys.\",\"supports\":\"Narrative irony and possessiveness complicate the relationship but are contextual features, not residual core force requiring O.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the avowal, while P captures the limited undertaking force supplied by the accompanying private vows. Emotional intensity and repetition can remain contextual delivery information without requiring E, and no important aspect of the utterance's core force falls outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension